{{- /*
component-guard — turn an optional run_once_ script into a no-op when its
component is disabled.

Call it right after `set -e` and after sourcing lib/logging.sh:

    {{ template "component-guard" (dict "name" "virtualization" "data" .) }}

To also undo what the script configured when the component is disabled, pass the
name of a teardown template (a file in .chezmoitemplates/):

    {{ template "component-guard" (dict "name" "looking_glass" "data" . "teardown" "looking-glass-teardown") }}

When the component is enabled the template emits nothing; when it is disabled it
emits log_skip + the teardown + exit 0, so the script still "runs" for chezmoi
but configures nothing. Packages are never removed: a disabled component stops
being installed, what is already there stays.

Use run_onchange_ (not run_once_) for a script that carries a teardown: run_once
keys its state on every content hash it has ever seen, so a second disable after
a re-enable would be a no-op. run_onchange compares with the last run instead.

Semantics mirror run_once_01-packages: a config that has no `components` key at
all keeps the pre-registry behaviour (every component except wine enabled), and
the legacy `wine = true` switch is still honoured for the wine component only.
*/ -}}
{{- $components := list -}}
{{- if hasKey .data "components" }}{{ $components = index .data "components" }}{{ end -}}
{{- $enabled := or (not (hasKey .data "components")) (has .name $components) -}}
{{- if and (eq .name "wine") (hasKey .data "wine") (index .data "wine") }}{{ $enabled = true }}{{ end -}}
{{- if not $enabled }}
log_skip "component '{{ .name }}' disabled — skipping"
{{- if .teardown }}
{{ includeTemplate .teardown . }}
{{- end }}
exit 0
{{- end }}
